.he "sum (1)                      9/30/26                      sum (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
sum
- compute check sums
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
sum [ -r ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Sum computes and prints a check sum and a block count for each named
file.  A block is 512 bytes, and the count is rounded up, so a file of
one byte and a file of five hundred both count one block and an empty
file counts none.
.sp
If no file names are given, sum reads the standard input, and prints
the check sum and the count with no name after them.
.sp
The check sum is a sixteen bit number, in the range 0 to 65535, that
depends on every byte of the file and on its position in the file.
Two files with the same check sum are very likely to be identical, so
a check sum read down a telephone is a way of asking whether a file
arrived intact:
.sp
.in +5
.br
sum a.out
.sp
.in -5
reads a number out; the person at the other end runs the same command
on the same file and compares.  A difference means the file changed,
and it says nothing about where - cmp (1) is what finds that.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-r	Descend the file hierarchy, summing each file found under a
	named directory.  A directory and a special file are not
	summed; only regular files are.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLE
.in +5
.br
.in +5
.br
.bd
sum -r /usr/src
.sp
The check sum of every file under /usr/src, each with the name it was
found under.
.in -5
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The block count is in 512 byte blocks.  Some other systems count a
block as 1024 bytes, and read the count on a file of six hundred
bytes as one where this one says two.
.sp
The 1982 micronix /bin/sum does not compute this check sum.  It adds
the file as sixteen bit little endian words, so it answers 6067 where
this command and the host sum answer 47926 for the same file, and a
sum from it cannot be checked against one from here.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
cmp (1), diff (1), wc (1)
